module nupp.host

--[[
`nupp.host` asks whatever is running the program for something and waits for
the answer: the page's JavaScript in a browser, or the C or Rust application
that embeds the runtime natively. Reach for it when a library needs a facility
the host has and Nupp does not, such as a canvas, a clipboard or an engine's
asset loader, and should not care which host it is running under.

A request names a *kind*, a dotted name the host answers, and carries a flat
list of values. Declare the kind's signature once as a function type and bind
it; callers then get named arguments and a checked call:

```nupp:fragment
local host = nupp.host

--- Returns the host's id for the decoded image, and its width and height.
local type DecodeImage = function(url: string, format: string?): (integer, integer, integer)
local type ReleaseImage = function(id: integer): nil

local releaseImage = host.bindPost<ReleaseImage>("tecs.image.release")
local decodeImage = host.bind<DecodeImage>("tecs.image.decode",
    late = function(id: any, ...: any): nil releaseImage(id) end)

local id, width, height = decodeImage(url = "sprites.png")
```

A call suspends through [](nupp.suspension) until the host answers, so it
composes with [task scopes](docs/learn/runtime/concurrency/task-scopes.md),
deadlines and cancellation. A host failure is raised in the caller with the
host's message. `bind` allocates its closure once; a call through it passes its
arguments straight through rather than gathering them into a table.

## Values

Arguments and results are each a count of flat values: `nil`, booleans,
numbers, strings and byte spans. Every position is kept, so interior and
trailing `nil`s arrive as written. A number must be finite and a string must be
UTF-8 text; arbitrary bytes are a [](nupp.mem.span) of bytes, never a string
holding binary. Tables and functions are refused, naming the argument's
position. Structured data is encoded into bytes by the library that owns it.

A byte span is a borrow, so it crosses through a bound kind whose signature
says so; `call` and `post` take their arguments as `any`, which a borrow cannot
cross, and carry the other values only:

```nupp:fragment
local host = nupp.host
local span = nupp.mem.span

local type Digest = function(borrows bytes: span.ByteSpan): (integer, span.ByteSpan)
local digest = host.bind<Digest>("app.digest")

const text = "hello"
local length, echoed = digest(bytes = span.fromString(text))
```

A byte result arrives as a byte span the caller owns. A string or byte value
has a size limit: a string at most 64 KiB, bytes sent to the host at most
64 MiB, and bytes answered at most 8 MiB. A host names its own resources with
integers it assigns.

## Results nobody is waiting for

A call cancelled after it reached the host may still be answered. When the
results name a host resource, declare `late`: it receives those results, runs
where no task is waiting, and must not suspend, so it releases through `post`,
which queues a request without waiting for it. A kind without `late` drops a
late answer.

`post` and `bindPost` never suspend. The host runs the request like any call;
its answer is discarded and its failure reported rather than raised.

## Streams

A stream carries messages one way, without answers. `send` appends one to a
kind's outbound stream, which ships with the frame it was sent in; `bindSend`
binds a kind to a typed function as `bind` does, which is how bytes are sent.
`route` delivers a kind's inbound messages as [](nupp.events) on a bus the
program owns, at an address it picks, with each message's values filling the
event's fields in declaration order:

```nupp:fragment
local host = nupp.host
local events = nupp.events

@derive(events.Event)
local struct PointerMove
    x: number
    y: number
end

const WINDOW = 1
local input: events.MessageBus<integer> = events.newMessageBus()
input:observe(WINDOW, PointerMove, |move| -> print(move.x, move.y), "cursor")
local route = host.route("app.pointer.move", PointerMove, input, WINDOW, "dropOldest", 64)
```

Messages arrive at a turn boundary, never in the middle of the code that runs
between them, and an event nothing observes is never built. A stream that
fills applies its policy: `block` suspends the sender until the frame ships,
`latest` keeps only the newest message, and `dropOldest` drops the oldest past
the limit and counts it. An inbound message carries scalars and strings, which
is what an event's fields hold; bytes travel on a call.

Natively a send reaches the application's handler at once, so nothing waits
to ship and a policy never applies.

## Kinds

A kind is two or more dot-separated segments of letters, digits, `_` and `-`,
such as `tecs.image.decode`. Names under `nupp.` are reserved for the runtime.

::: seealso
- [suspension.md](docs/learn/runtime/concurrency/suspension.md) for what a wait
  does under a handler
- [embedding.md](docs/learn/projects/embedding.md) for answering requests from C
:::
]]

local spi = require("nupp.spi")
local events = require("nupp.events")
local target = require("nupp.runtime.target")
local contracts = require("nupp.host.spi")

local provider: contracts.Provider = do
    const chosen = spi.select(spi.load(contracts.Provider))
    if chosen ~= nil then
        yield chosen
    end
    if target.host == "native" then
        yield _G.require("nupp.runtime.provider.nativehost") as contracts.Provider
    end
    yield _G.require("nupp.runtime.browser.host") as contracts.Provider
end

local host = {}

--- Called with the results of a call whose caller stopped waiting.
type host.Late = contracts.Late

--- What a full stream does with one more message.
type host.Policy = contracts.Policy

--- An inbound stream's delivery to a bus; close it to stop.
record host.Route
    @private stop: function(): nil
    @private droppedCount: function(): integer

    --- Stops delivering; messages already on their way are discarded.
    function close(self: host.Route): nil
        self.stop()
    end

    --- How many inbound messages this route has dropped under its policy.
    function dropped(self: host.Route): integer
        return self.droppedCount()
    end
end

const DEFAULT_LIMIT = 1024

local function checkedLimit(limit: integer?): integer
    if limit == nil then
        return DEFAULT_LIMIT
    end
    if limit % 1 ~= 0 or limit < 1 then
        error("nupp: a host stream limit must be a positive integer", 3)
    end

    return limit
end

const MAX_KIND_BYTES = 128

local function checkedKind(kind: string): string
    if type(kind) ~= "string" then
        error("nupp: a host kind must be a string, not " .. type(kind), 3)
    end
    if #kind > MAX_KIND_BYTES then
        error("nupp: host kind " .. kind:sub(1, 32) .. "... is longer than 128 bytes", 3)
    end
    if not kind:match("^[%w_%-]+%.[%w_%-%.]*[%w_%-]$") or kind:find("..", 1, true) then
        error("nupp: host kind " .. string.format("%q", kind) .. " must be dot-separated names", 3)
    end
    if kind:sub(1, 5) == "nupp." then
        error("nupp: host kind " .. kind .. " is reserved for the runtime", 3)
    end

    return kind
end

--- Reports whether a host is attached to answer requests.
---
--- Natively that means an embedding application; a program run on its own has
--- no host. In a browser the page is always attached, though it may answer a
--- kind it does not know with a failure.
--- @export
function host.attached(): boolean
    return provider.attached()
end

--- Sends a request of `kind` and waits for the host to answer it.
---
--- @param kind the dotted name the host answers
--- @return the host's results, each position kept
--- @raises string when a value cannot cross, no host answers the kind, or the
---     host fails the request
--- @export
function host.call(kind: string, ...: any): ...any
    return provider.call(checkedKind(kind), nil, ...)
end

--- Binds `kind` to a function of type `F` that calls it.
---
--- `F` supplies the parameter names and result types callers see, so a bound
--- kind takes named arguments and checks them. Nothing checks that the host's
--- answer matches `F`'s results.
---
--- @param kind the dotted name the host answers
--- @param late receives the results of a call answered after its caller was
---     cancelled; it must not suspend
--- @return a function calling `kind` with its arguments
--- @export
function host.bind<F>(kind: string, late: contracts.Late?): F
    local checked = checkedKind(kind)
    local call = provider.call
    local bound = function(...: any): ...any
        return call(checked, late, ...)
    end

    return bound as F
end

--- Queues a request of `kind` and returns without waiting for its answer.
---
--- @param kind the dotted name the host answers
--- @raises string when a value cannot cross or too many posts are pending
--- @export
function host.post(kind: string, ...: any): nil
    provider.post(checkedKind(kind), ...)
end

--- Binds `kind` to a function of type `F` that posts it.
---
--- @param kind the dotted name the host answers
--- @return a function posting `kind` with its arguments
--- @export
function host.bindPost<F>(kind: string): F
    local checked = checkedKind(kind)
    local post = provider.post
    local bound = function(...: any): nil
        post(checked, ...)
    end

    return bound as F
end

--- Sends a message on `kind`'s outbound stream.
---
--- @param kind the dotted name the host answers
--- @raises string when a value cannot cross
--- @export
function host.send(kind: string, ...: any): nil
    provider.send(checkedKind(kind), "block", DEFAULT_LIMIT, ...)
end

--- Binds `kind`'s outbound stream to a function of type `F` that sends on it.
---
--- @param kind the dotted name the host answers
--- @param policy what a full stream does with one more message, `block` unless named
--- @param limit how many unshipped messages make the stream full, 1024 unless named
--- @return a function sending on `kind` with its arguments
--- @export
function host.bindSend<F>(kind: string, policy: contracts.Policy?, limit: integer?): F
    local checked = checkedKind(kind)
    local selected = policy or "block"
    local bound = checkedLimit(limit)
    local send = provider.send

    local function sender(...: any): nil
        send(checked, selected, bound, ...)
    end

    return sender as F
end

--- Delivers `kind`'s inbound messages to `bus` as `event`s at `address`.
---
--- Addresses are integers, the ids a host gives its windows, pads and devices.
---
--- @param kind the dotted name the host pushes
--- @param event the event each message fills, its fields in declaration order
--- @param policy `latest` or `dropOldest`; `dropOldest` unless named
--- @param limit how many messages one turn delivers, 1024 unless named
--- @return the route, which stops when closed
--- @raises string when `policy` is `block`, which an inbound stream cannot do
--- @export
function host.route<E is events.Emittable>(
    kind: string,
    event: Type<E>,
    bus: events.MessageBus<integer>,
    address: integer,
    policy: contracts.Policy?,
    limit: integer?
): host.Route
    local checked = checkedKind(kind)
    local selected = policy or "dropOldest"
    if selected ~= "latest" and selected ~= "dropOldest" then
        error("nupp: an inbound host stream cannot block the host; route it latest or dropOldest", 2)
    end
    local emit = events.emit as any

    local function deliver(...: any): nil
        emit(bus, address, event, ...)
    end

    local stop, dropped = provider.route(checked, selected, checkedLimit(limit), deliver)

    return new host.Route(stop = stop, droppedCount = dropped)
end

export = host
